use .

export editor Rhun:
    url: https://github.com/vshvedov/rhun/
    syntaxHighlighter: [rhun-grammar, lsp]

    highlighting:
        A rhun grammar (a `.syn` file) gives the syntax highlighting. It claims
        `.pi` files, gives `//` to Toggle Comment and indents the line after a
        colon. A rhun grammar has no context, so it colours a word by its
        spelling alone. Thus the grammar lists no keywords, and it does not give
        the keyword colour to a word in a string body: `from` in prose is plain.
        The language server gives the keyword colour to the keywords at column 0
        and in a declaration header, where the parser reads them as keywords,
        through its semantic tokens. It also gives the colours that need the
        resolved program. Every line where the grammar and the parser disagree
        is a known, listed gap, as `oneClassification` asks.

    languageServer:
        rhun talks to its extensions in its own protocol, not in LSP. The Piton
        extension for rhun is the first-party rhun-editor/lsp bridge, changed to
        serve Piton files only and to start `piton lsp`. It gives rhun the full
        LSP support of `piton lsp`: problems, completion, hover, navigation,
        rename, formatting, folding, semantic colours and inlay hints.

        The extension shows what the server shows, so it shows no JSON hints
        when the build writes no JSON. A Belay project that sets no `output` or
        `renderer` writes no JSON. There, no hover, code lens or inlay hint
        names a JSON file or shows a value as JSON. They name the files of the
        Belay targets.
